{The opening was a Four Knights Opening and the first one decided. "I played H. E. Atkins and lost owing to my opponent's subtle and almost ungentlemanly manoeuvres ground-baited with innocent looking and apparently digestible pawns." (Letter, GA Heginbottom to BCM, March 1939, p112)} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bb5 Bb4 5. O-O O-O 6. d3 d6 7. Bg5 Be6 8. Ne2 Ne7 9. Ng3 Ne8 10. Bxe8 Qxe8 11. Be3 f5 12. c3 Ba5 13. exf5 Nxf5 14. Nxf5 Rxf5 15. Nd2 Bb6 16. b3 Qg6 17. Nf3 Rh5 18. Bxb6 axb6 19. Kh1 Bg4 20. Rg1 Rf8 21. b4 Rxf3 22. gxf3 Rxh2+ 23. Kxh2 Qh5+ 24. Kg2 Qh3# {Source: Chess Monthly, Vol. 15, p238; London Evening Standard, 19 March 1894} 0-1

