[BlackTeam "Cambridge University"]

{"Mr Sergeant, at board no.3, opened with a Ruy Lopez, in which pieces were exchanged early, and some brisk play ensued when he lost a piece, leaving the following position for adjudication. Black's last move was 28...RxKt, thereby gaining a clear piece, but an absolute win is not easily to be demonstrated, and Mr Hoffer gave it in as drawn." Source: BCM, April 1894, p148-9.} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 d6 4. Bxc6+ bxc6 5. d4 exd4 6. Qxd4 Nf6 7. e5 c5 8. Qd3 Nd7 9. O-O Be7 10. Bf4 Nb6 11. exd6 cxd6 12. Rd1 O-O 13. Nc3 Be6 14. Bxd6 Bxd6 15. Qxd6 Qxd6 16. Rxd6 Nc4 17. Rc6 Nxb2 18. Rxc5 Nc4 19. Nd4 Nb6 20. Nxe6 fxe6 21. Re1 Rac8 22. Ne4 Rxc5 23. Nxc5 Rc8 24. Re5 Nc4 25. Rg5 h6 26. Rh5 g6 27. Rxh6 Kg7 28. Rh4 Rxc5 {Some confusion here - this is the position for adjudication according to BCM, April 1894, p149, but Chess Monthly has considerably more moves as follows...} 29. g3 Nd2 30. Ra4 a5 31. Kg2 g5 32. h3 Nc4 33. c3 Nb6 34. Rg4 Kf6 35. h4 gxh4 36. Rxh4 Rxc3 37. Rh6+ Kf7 38. Rh7+ Kf6 39. Rh6+ Kf7 40. Rh7+ Kg6 41. Ra7 a4 42. Ra6 Rc6 43. g4 Kg5 44. Kg3 e5 45. Ra5 Kf6 46. Ra6 Rd6 47. f3 Ke6 {adjudicated by Hoffer, who says in his notes in 'Chess Monthly': "Mr Sergeant, who made such a gallant stand after losing a piece, was given the benefit of the doubt." [The latter moves were not recorded, so the position is taken down partly from memory - ed]. Source: Chess Monthly, Vol. 15, p240} 1/2-1/2

